       This documentation is primarily aimed at provider authors. See
       pprroovviiddeerr(7) for further information.

       The MAC operation enables providers to implement mac algorithms and
       make them available to applications via the API functions
       EEVVPP__MMAACC__iinniitt(3), EEVVPP__MMAACC__uuppddaattee(3) and EEVVPP__MMAACC__ffiinnaall(3).

       All "functions" mentioned here are passed as function pointers between
       _l_i_b_c_r_y_p_t_o and the provider in OOSSSSLL__DDIISSPPAATTCCHH(3) arrays via
       OOSSSSLL__AALLGGOORRIITTHHMM(3) arrays that are returned by the provider’s
       pprroovviiddeerr__qquueerryy__ooppeerraattiioonn(()) function (see "Provider Functions" in
       pprroovviiddeerr--bbaassee(7)).

       All these "functions" have a corresponding function type definition
       named OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__{{nnaammee}}__ffnn, and a helper function to retrieve the
       function pointer from an OOSSSSLL__DDIISSPPAATTCCHH(3) element named
       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__{{nnaammee}}.  For example, the "function" OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__nneewwccttxx(())
       has these:

        typedef void *(OSSL_FUNC_mac_newctx_fn)(void *provctx);
        static ossl_inline OSSL_FUNC_mac_newctx_fn
            OSSL_FUNC_mac_newctx(const OSSL_DISPATCH *opf);

       OOSSSSLL__DDIISSPPAATTCCHH(3) arrays are indexed by numbers that are provided as
       macros in ooppeennssssll--ccoorree__ddiissppaattcchh..hh(7), as follows:

        OSSL_FUNC_mac_newctx               OSSL_FUNC_MAC_NEWCTX
        OSSL_FUNC_mac_freectx              OSSL_FUNC_MAC_FREECTX
        OSSL_FUNC_mac_dupctx               OSSL_FUNC_MAC_DUPCTX

        OSSL_FUNC_mac_init                 OSSL_FUNC_MAC_INIT
        OSSL_FUNC_mac_update               OSSL_FUNC_MAC_UPDATE
        OSSL_FUNC_mac_final                OSSL_FUNC_MAC_FINAL

        OSSL_FUNC_mac_get_params           OSSL_FUNC_MAC_GET_PARAMS
        OSSL_FUNC_mac_get_ctx_params       OSSL_FUNC_MAC_GET_CTX_PARAMS
        OSSL_FUNC_mac_set_ctx_params       OSSL_FUNC_MAC_SET_CTX_PARAMS

        OSSL_FUNC_mac_gettable_params      OSSL_FUNC_MAC_GETTABLE_PARAMS
        OSSL_FUNC_mac_gettable_ctx_params  OSSL_FUNC_MAC_GETTABLE_CTX_PARAMS
        OSSL_FUNC_mac_settable_ctx_params  OSSL_FUNC_MAC_SETTABLE_CTX_PARAMS

       A mac algorithm implementation may not implement all of these
       functions.  In order to be a consistent set of functions, at least the
       following functions must be implemented: OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__nneewwccttxx(()),
       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__ffrreeeeccttxx(()), OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__iinniitt(()), OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__uuppddaattee(()),
       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__ffiinnaall(()).  All other functions are optional.

   CCoonntteexxtt MMaannaaggeemmeenntt FFuunnccttiioonnss
       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__nneewwccttxx(()) should create and return a pointer to a provider
       side structure for holding context information during a mac operation.
       A pointer to this context will be passed back in a number of the other
       mac operation function calls.  The parameter _p_r_o_v_c_t_x is the provider
       context generated during provider initialisation (see pprroovviiddeerr(7)).

       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__ffrreeeeccttxx(()) is passed a pointer to the provider side mac
       context in the _m_c_t_x parameter.  If it receives NULL as _m_c_t_x value, it
       should not do anything other than return.  This function should free
       any resources associated with that context.

       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__dduuppccttxx(()) should duplicate the provider side mac context
       in the _m_c_t_x parameter and return the duplicate copy.

   EEnnccrryyppttiioonn//DDeeccrryyppttiioonn FFuunnccttiioonnss
       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__iinniitt(()) initialises a mac operation given a newly created
       provider side mac context in the _m_c_t_x parameter.  The _p_a_r_a_m_s are set
       before setting the MAC _k_e_y of _k_e_y_l_e_n bytes.

       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__uuppddaattee(()) is called to supply data for MAC computation of
       a previously initialised mac operation.  The _m_c_t_x parameter contains a
       pointer to a previously initialised provider side context.
       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__uuppddaattee(()) may be called multiple times for a single mac
       operation.

       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__ffiinnaall(()) completes the MAC computation started through
       previous OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__iinniitt(()) and OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__uuppddaattee(()) calls.  The
       _m_c_t_x parameter contains a pointer to the provider side context.  The
       resulting MAC should be written to _o_u_t and the amount of data written
       to _*_o_u_t_l, which should not exceed _o_u_t_s_i_z_e bytes.  The same expectations
       apply to _o_u_t_s_i_z_e as documented for EEVVPP__MMAACC__ffiinnaall(3).

   MMaacc PPaarraammeetteerrss
       See OOSSSSLL__PPAARRAAMM(3) for further details on the parameters structure used
       by these functions.

       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__ggeett__ppaarraammss(()) gets details of parameter values associated
       with the provider algorithm and stores them in _p_a_r_a_m_s.

       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__sseett__ccttxx__ppaarraammss(()) sets mac parameters associated with the
       given provider side mac context _m_c_t_x to _p_a_r_a_m_s.  Any parameter settings
       are additional to any that were previously set.  Passing NULL for
       _p_a_r_a_m_s should return true.

       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__ggeett__ccttxx__ppaarraammss(()) gets details of currently set parameter
       values associated with the given provider side mac context _m_c_t_x and
       stores them in _p_a_r_a_m_s.  Passing NULL for _p_a_r_a_m_s should return true.

       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__ggeettttaabbllee__ppaarraammss(()), OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__ggeettttaabbllee__ccttxx__ppaarraammss(()),
       and OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__sseettttaabbllee__ccttxx__ppaarraammss(()) all return constant
       OOSSSSLL__PPAARRAAMM(3) arrays as descriptors of the parameters that
       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__ggeett__ppaarraammss(()), OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__ggeett__ccttxx__ppaarraammss(()), and
       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__sseett__ccttxx__ppaarraammss(()) can handle, respectively.
       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__ggeettttaabbllee__ccttxx__ppaarraammss(()) and
       OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__sseettttaabbllee__ccttxx__ppaarraammss(()) will return the parameters
       associated with the provider side context _m_c_t_x in its current state if
       it is not NULL.  Otherwise, they return the parameters associated with
       the provider side algorithm _p_r_o_v_c_t_x.

       All MAC implementations are expected to handle the following
       parameters:

       with OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__sseett__ccttxx__ppaarraammss(()):
           "key" (OOSSSSLL__MMAACC__PPAARRAAMM__KKEEYY) <octet string>
               Sets  the  key in the associated MAC ctx.  This is identical to
               passing a _k_e_y argument to the OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__mmaacc__iinniitt(()) function.

       with OOSSSSLL__FFUUNNCC__ggeett__ppaarraammss(()):
           "size" (OOSSSSLL__MMAACC__PPAARRAAMM__SSIIZZEE) <integer>
               Can be used to get the default MAC size  (which  might  be  the
               only allowable MAC size for the implementation).

               Note  that some implementations allow setting the size that the
               resulting MAC should have as well, see the documentation of the
               implementation.

           "size" (OOSSSSLL__MMAACC__PPAARRAAMM__BBLLOOCCKK__SSIIZZEE) <integer>
               Can be used to get the MAC block  size  (if  supported  by  the
               algorithm).

NNOOTTEESS
       The  MAC  life‐cycle  is  described  in  lliiffee__ccyyccllee--rraanndd(7).  Providers
       should ensure that the various transitions listed there are  supported.
       At   some   point  the  EVP  layer  will  begin  enforcing  the  listed
       transitions.

